jt-pve-storage-dellemc (0.8.23~beta1-1) unstable; urgency=medium

  * BETA RELEASE. Not for production use.
  * Added: 'dell-host-mode host-group' (PowerStore only), which keeps the
    per-node host objects and also puts them in an array host group named
    after the cluster, so one mapping reaches every node. Requested by
    Alexander Gott (@alexandergott-afk) in issue #5, who also settled what to
    do about a host that is already in a group. A host belongs to at most one
    host group and is mapped THROUGH it, so a host already in somebody else's
    group is left there and reported once: moving it would take away every
    volume that group maps to the node. Removal is never automatic and only
    groups this plugin created are ever deleted.
  * Added: the cluster name is now read from /etc/pve/corosync.conf when a
    storage is ADDED and written into its configuration, so nobody has to
    remember --dell-cluster-name. Existing storages are deliberately left
    alone, because the name is part of the host object name and re-deriving it
    at activation would make a storage stop finding its host. Issue #4, and
    the reporter confirmed no migration is wanted.
  * The failure message for a name the array refuses while its listing shows
    it free now names the object holding it. The reporter supplied the
    endpoints: /recycle_bin exists since PowerStore 3.5.0.0, even though
    python-powerstore does not wrap it, which is what an earlier version of
    this plugin wrongly took as proof there was nothing to ask. Read only: the
    recycle bin exists so that deletion is deliberate.
  * Removed a dead encode_host_group_name that named a host group the same as
    the shared-mode HOST object. Nothing called it but a test, and it dated
    from the belief corrected in 0.8.22.

 -- Jason Cheng <jason@jason.tools>  Tue, 25 Aug 2026 12:00:00 +0800

jt-pve-storage-dellemc (0.8.22~beta1-1) unstable; urgency=low

  * BETA RELEASE. Not for production use.
  * Corrected: 'dell-host-mode shared' was described, in the option's own
    schema text and in docs/CONFIGURATION.md, as registering "a single host
    group for the whole cluster". It does not. It registers one HOST OBJECT
    and puts every node's initiators into it, which is a different thing on
    every one of these arrays. Raised by Alexander Gott (@alexandergott-afk)
    in issue #5, who read the behaviour correctly while the documentation did
    not.
  * The documentation now also says what IS available today: the plugin does
    not create array host groups, but it does map through one that already
    exists, because a host that is a member of a group can only be mapped
    through the group. An operator who builds the group themselves gets one
    mapping covering the cluster.
  * t/06-blockbase.t pins the behaviour, rather than guarding the prose: a
    test that reads documentation for meaning ends up guessing at intent.

 -- Jason Cheng <jason@jason.tools>  Tue, 25 Aug 2026 10:00:00 +0800

jt-pve-storage-dellemc (0.8.21~beta1-1) unstable; urgency=low

  * BETA RELEASE. Not for production use.
  * Corrected, in the code comments and in the 0.8.19 changelog entry: the
    kernel's "LUN assignments on this target have changed" is NOT evidence of
    a stale sd path. It is the unit attention an array raises whenever its LUN
    inventory changes, so this plugin causes one on every map and unmap, and
    so does every other array. A node with only NetApp iSCSI and no Dell
    storage at all has logged it 396 times in sixty days. The reporter of
    issue #7 came back with the message still appearing, which is how the
    wrong explanation was caught.
  * The cleanup added in 0.8.19 stands and was worth making, for a different
    reason than was given: _release_volume left sd paths that still describe
    the volume which used to be at that LUN id, and next_free_lun reuses the
    lowest free id, so a different volume arrives there. That is the hazard;
    the kernel message was never its indicator.

