jt-pve-storage-dellemc (0.8.19~beta1-1) unstable; urgency=high

  * BETA RELEASE. Not for production use.
  * Fixed: newly mapped LUNs never got a multipath map on a node using the
    Debian and Proxmox default 'find_multipaths strict'. Under that setting
    multipathd builds a map only for a WWID already listed in
    /etc/multipath/wwids, however many healthy paths there are, and nothing
    wrote that entry. multipath_claim_wwid now runs 'multipath -a <wwid>'
    before offering the paths, which claims one WWID and leaves the node's
    policy alone. Reported on a PowerStore over FC by Alexander Gott
    (@alexandergott-afk) in issue #6; PowerVault and Unity had the same gap.
  * Fixed: the diagnostic for that case named find_multipaths but described
    the wrong mechanism, telling the operator to check for a second path when
    'strict' does not care how many paths there are. It now separates strict
    from yes and smart, and reports whether the WWID is in the wwids file.
  * Fixed: _release_volume, the delete path for config backup volumes and
    temporary snapshot clones, did no local device cleanup at all. The sd
    paths it left behind are what produce the kernel's "LUN assignments on
    this target have changed" once a freed LUN id is reused. cleanup_lun_
    devices already existed and this path simply never called it. Reported as
    issue #7. The other messages in that report are the deliberate cost of
    unmapping before cleaning up, which is what stops an in-flight rescan
    rebuilding the device.
  * Fixed: a volume in a group this plugin did not create could never be
    deleted. PowerStore refuses to delete a volume that is still a member,
    confirmed by the reporter in issue #3, so removal is mandatory. The
    delete path now removes the volume from every group it is in, while a
    rename still leaves an operator's own group alone.
  * docs/TESTING.md: the customer's PowerStore is on Fibre Channel, so that
    data path is verified on two families now and iSCSI on PowerStore is
    recorded as entirely unrun.

 -- Jason Cheng <jason@jason.tools>  Mon, 24 Aug 2026 20:00:00 +0800

jt-pve-storage-dellemc (0.8.18~beta1-1) unstable; urgency=medium

  * BETA RELEASE. Not for production use.
  * Added: 'pstore-volume-group-per-vm', off by default, which gives each VM a
    PowerStore volume group of its own so that protection policies and
    consistent group snapshots can be applied per VM from PowerStore Manager.
    Requested by Alexander Gott (@alexandergott-afk) in issue #3, who also
    settled the design question about what happens when a disk is reassigned
    to another VM. A volume belongs to at most one volume group, so this is
    mutually exclusive with 'pstore-volume-group' and refused at pvesm add or
    pvesm set rather than discovered later. It never fails a disk creation, it
    only takes in VM disks (not the config backup volume or the temporary
    snapshot clones), membership follows the VMID and leaves the old group
    first, and cleanup runs even after the option is switched off. An empty
    group is deleted only when this plugin created it, the array answered and
    answered empty, and no protection policy is attached.
  * Documentation: the site and both READMEs said one array had run this. Two
    have. A PowerStore has run volume create, mapping, device discovery,
    snapshot creation and guests running off it, which also moves six rows of
    docs/TESTING.md from NOT VERIFIED to verified or partly verified.
  * Documentation: twelve places said there are three storage types. There are
    four. t/16-docs.t now takes the number from the plugin classes.
  * Fixed: every release page said that 0.x releases are marked Pre-release
    and that /releases/latest would not resolve. Neither is true, and the
    workflow publishes a fixed-name asset precisely so that URL works. The
    release notes now give it, as the README always has.

 -- Jason Cheng <jason@jason.tools>  Mon, 24 Aug 2026 17:30:00 +0800

jt-pve-storage-dellemc (0.8.17~beta1-1) unstable; urgency=high

  * BETA RELEASE. Not for production use.
  * Fixed: taking a snapshot of a running VM froze the guest for about 8
    seconds. volume_snapshot runs between PVE's guest-fsfreeze-freeze and its
    thaw, so everything it does is time the guest does no I/O - and the VM
    configuration backup, which is on by default, creates a volume on the
    array, maps it, rescans the transport, waits for a multipath device,
    makes a filesystem and mounts it. The array snapshot itself took 0.00s.
    The configuration copy now runs in a detached background process, so the
    freeze covers only the array snapshot. Reported with timestamps proving
    where the time went by Alexander Gott (@alexandergott-afk) in issue #2.
  * dell-config-backup-timeout no longer lengthens the guest freeze; its
    description said it kept a slow fabric from stalling a snapshot, which
    invited raising it, and its maximum of 60 reached qemu-guest-agent's own
    fsfreeze timeout.
  * No config backup is made beside a vzdump snapshot, which is deleted
    within the same backup run, and only one background process is started
    per snapshot rather than one per disk.
  * Fixed: the documentation offered SAS as a PowerVault data path and filed
    it under things awaiting hardware. It is not implemented and cannot be
    configured - dell-protocol's enum is iscsi, fc, sdc, nvme. Every document
    now says so, and t/16-docs.t fails any data-path cell naming a protocol
    the enum does not carry.

 -- Jason Cheng <jason@jason.tools>  Fri, 21 Aug 2026 19:30:00 +0800

jt-pve-storage-dellemc (0.8.16~beta1-1) unstable; urgency=high

  * BETA RELEASE. Not for production use.
  * Fixed: PowerStore refused every OVMF EFI disk, so no UEFI guest could be
    migrated or cloned onto the storage while its ordinary disks went across
    without complaint. PVE allocates an EFI disk at the size of
    OVMF_VARS_4M.fd - 540672 bytes - and PowerStore rejects any volume below
    1 MiB. 540672 is already an exact multiple of the array.s 8 KiB
    granularity, so rounding had nothing to say about it and the size reached
    the array unchanged. align_size now lifts a request to the array minimum
    before it rounds. Reported with the array.s own refusal quoted, and with
    a working fix, by Alexander Gott (@alexandergott-afk) in GitHub issue #1.
  * Unity has had this floor since it was written; PowerVault and PowerFlex
    have granularities above 1 MiB and were never exposed. The guard is now
    stated once per family in t/13-hostile.t, which asks every family what it
    does with a 540672-byte request rather than only the one that failed.

 -- Jason Cheng <jason@jason.tools>  Fri, 21 Aug 2026 16:00:00 +0800

jt-pve-storage-dellemc (0.8.15~beta1-1) unstable; urgency=high

  * BETA RELEASE. Not for production use.
  * Fixed: the logout was sent without the session key, so the array ended
    nothing - every logout since 0.8.9 was a no-op. _release_request passes
    no_auth => 1 to avoid recursing through ensure_session, and no_auth also
    skips _auth_headers, which is where the session key lives. The logout now
    carries the credential explicitly, for every family. This is why 0.8.14
    tripled the leak rather than fixing it: it correctly stopped a forked
    child from caching, so a poll went from one client to three, and with the
    logout ineffective from one leaked session per poll to three.
  * The test fixture now refuses a logout that does not carry a valid session
    key, as the array does; accepting one without looking at the headers is
    why this survived five releases.

