jt-pve-storage-dellemc (0.7.72~beta1-1) unstable; urgency=medium

  * BETA RELEASE. Not for production use.
  * UNITY: the pool key on a create was wrong, in the shape of lesson 28
    again. Dell's LunParameters struct names its field StoragePool; the JSON
    tag that actually goes on the wire is 'pool'. An earlier release read
    the field name and sent 'storagePool' - every create would have been
    refused, or accepted with the pool silently ignored. In Go code the
    json tag is the property name and the field name is a printed one. The
    emulator did not catch it because it accepts any body.
  * UNITY: a concurrent mapping change could silently unmap a node.
    hostAccess is read-modify-write with no compare-and-swap: two nodes
    writing at once each read the list, each write their version, and the
    second write discards the first. The node whose entry was lost believes
    it is mapped and its device never appears; on a migration that is the
    running guest's disk. Attach and detach now verify the write and retry
    with a fresh read; a write that never survives is an error naming the
    likely cause.
  * UNITY: resize double-checked key-for-key against Dell's own client -
    same URI, same lunParameters/size wrapper, new total rather than a
    delta. Rename's bare name body matches too.

 -- Jason Cheng <jason@jason.tools>  Thu, 06 Aug 2026 02:30:00 +0800

jt-pve-storage-dellemc (0.7.71~beta1-1) unstable; urgency=medium

  * BETA RELEASE. Not for production use.
  * UNITY: every rollback would eventually have made the volume undeletable.
    Dell's white paper is explicit that a restore automatically creates a
    backup snapshot, whether or not one was asked for. Left to the array it
    gets a name the snapshot purge does not recognise and the ownership gate
    refuses to touch; Unity then refuses to delete a LUN that still has
    snapshots, so from the first qm rollback on, qm destroy fails - days
    later, with nothing pointing back at the cause. The restore now passes
    copyName, naming the backup in this plugin's own scheme with room for
    the counter Unity appends on the second rollback. The lifecycle fake
    creates a backup snapshot on every restore the way the array does, and
    the tests prove the volume deletes after one rollback and after two.
  * UNITY: an EFI disk could not have been created. PVE asks for 4 MiB
    volumes (EFI disk, TPM state) and Unisphere refuses a LUN below its
    minimum, taking the whole qm create with it. Requests below 1 GiB are
    rounded up.
  * UNITY: the whole PVE feature matrix is now asserted per volume kind -
    snapshot and rename of a linked clone, clone from template and from
    snapshot, template conversion, copy, raw+size export, container
    fsfreeze. A wrong 'no' there is invisible: the button simply is not
    there.

