jt-pve-storage-dellemc (0.7.70~beta1-1) unstable; urgency=medium

  * BETA RELEASE. Not for production use.
  * UNITY: a 302 would have been followed, and on this API it is not a
    redirect. Dell's own status table gives 302 as Unauthorized - the
    X-EMC-REST-CLIENT header missing or not true. LWP follows up to seven
    redirects by default, so the client would have fetched the array's web
    UI, got HTML and reported "the body is not JSON", naming the symptom and
    hiding the cause. Redirects are now refused outright; every request
    carries an Authorization header, and following one is also how a
    credential reaches a host nobody chose. 401 is the same error with the
    header present, so it is now explained as bad credentials.
  * UNITY: paging rested on a guess. A collection was walked until a page
    came back shorter than requested, and the array is free to return fewer
    rows than asked for. with_entrycount=true makes it report how many are in
    the complete list; the short-page rule is now only the fallback for a
    firmware that reports no count. A silently truncated listing is how the
    orphan reaper comes to treat live volumes as deleted.
  * UNITY: an error body is {error: {errorCode, httpStatusCode, messages}}
    and errorCode is a number - the stable thing to key on, as PowerVault's
    return code is. It is now read out; the messages beside it are localised
    into nine languages and are for a human to read. 409 and 422 gained an
    explanation of what the array meant.

