jt-pve-storage-dellemc (0.7.68~beta1-1) unstable; urgency=medium

  * BETA RELEASE. Not for production use.
  * NEW STORAGE TYPE: dellunity, for Dell EMC Unity XT. A customer has a
    Unity 480 on Fibre Channel, so this family is written against hardware
    that exists. One VM disk is one Unity LUN, with the array's own snapshots
    and thin clones; dm-multipath, device discovery and every safety check
    are the shared layer's, already exercised on an ME4024 over FC.
  * NOTHING IN THIS FAMILY HAS BEEN RUN AGAINST A UNITY ARRAY, but very
    little of it is guessed: every URI, request body and field list comes
    from github.com/dell/gounity, the client Dell's own CSI driver uses,
    rather than from documentation prose. Where the two disagreed the code
    won - a Unisphere page gives the LUN name limit as 85 characters and
    Dell's client refuses anything over 63 before the request reaches the
    array.
  * Unity answers a lookup by name directly, at /instances/lun/name:<name>,
    so this family carries none of the "an empty filtered listing means two
    things" defences the others need.
  * hostAccess REPLACES the host list rather than adding to it. Mapping reads
    the current list and sends the union, unmapping sends the difference, and
    both read inside the caller's retry loop. Sending only this node's host
    is how a volume gets unmapped from every other node in the cluster.
  * A LUN is read as 'lun' and acted on as 'storageResource'; the pool key on
    a create is 'storagePool'. A linked clone is a thin clone of a snapshot,
    so a template's marker outlives its clones. Sizes are bytes.
  * 120 new tests, none of which need an array: 85 for the client and 35
    walking a whole VM's life against a fake array that refuses what a real
    one refuses. Writing them found a defect - fields are opt-in on this API,
    so a pool that comes back as an empty shell looks exactly like one that
    was found, and the create would have sent a null where the pool id goes.
  * docs/TESTING.md records every field with its provenance and the five
    questions only an array can answer, including the SCSI vendor and product
    strings that decide whether a device is recognised at all.

