jt-pve-storage-dellemc (0.7.65~beta1-1) unstable; urgency=medium

  * BETA RELEASE. Not for production use.
  * FIRST END-TO-END RUN ON REAL HARDWARE. A customer took 0.7.64 to a
    PowerVault ME4024 (MIL-ME4024, firmware GT280R011-01, Fibre Channel) and
    walked the whole of docs/FIRST_RUN. Three defects sat between the storage
    coming up and a working disk, each hidden by the one before it.
  * POWERVAULT: no volume could be mapped, -10386. 'map' and 'unmap' take an
    IDENTIFIER, and the ME grammar distinguishes three kinds of object by a
    suffix: <name> is an initiator, <name>.* a host, <name>.*.* a host group.
    The bare host name was sent, so the array looked for an initiator by that
    name and correctly did not find one. Both directions changed together:
    the array reports the same grammar back in 'show maps', so a comparison
    that does not drop the suffix never matches, and fixing only the sending
    side turns "cannot map" into "LUN collision".
  * POWERVAULT: the second volume collided, -3177. 'show maps' answers with a
    tree grouped by volume - the top level is 'volume-view', one per volume,
    each carrying its rows under 'volume-view-mappings'. There is no
    top-level array of mappings, so the list came back empty, every LUN
    looked free, and the second volume was given an id the first held. Each
    row now carries down the volume it belongs to. This is the same mistake
    as the host listing in 0.7.63, in a different listing of the same array.
  * POWERVAULT: every delete tried to unmap 'all other initiators', -10007.
    Every volume carries one row describing its default mapping, present even
    when there is none: lun "", access "not-mapped", access-numeric 0,
    identifier "all other initiators", nickname "". It is not in the CLI's
    table, only in the JSON, and the empty nickname promoted that display
    string into the name list. It is now recognised by its access rather than
    by the string, which a display layer chose and may translate.
  * POWERVAULT: a mapping recorded at host-group level now counts as using
    its LUN. The array records one there even when 'map' named a host, if
    that host is the group's only member, and such a row holds the LUN
    against every host in the group.
  * POWERVAULT: 'show maps <volume>' is checked to have filtered. The rows
    now carry their volume, so the check is free; on the unmap path an
    unchecked answer means reporting a volume mapped when it is not.
  * VERIFIED on that array: 'unmap volume initiator <host>.* <volume>' (the
    source had it marked NOT VERIFIED); the WWID rule, whose result matched
    multipath -ll and /dev/mapper on four volumes; the bare-hex WWPN
    spelling; that a percent-encoded '*' is decoded before the CLI sees it;
    and every item in docs/FIRST_RUN. This does not make the other two
    families, or iSCSI, or SAS, verified.

 -- Jason Cheng <jason@jason.tools>  Wed, 05 Aug 2026 15:00:00 +0800

jt-pve-storage-dellemc (0.7.64~beta1-1) unstable; urgency=medium

  * BETA RELEASE. Not for production use.
  * VERIFIED: the ME4024 payloads behind 0.7.63 are now on file. A customer
    supplied the array's actual 'show host-groups' and 'show pools'
    responses (PowerVault ME4024, MIL-ME4024, firmware GT280R011-01, Fibre
    Channel). Both fixes released in 0.7.63 were written from Dell's
    documentation and match what the array sends: the top level carries only
    'host-group', hosts nest under 'host' (singular) and initiators under
    'initiator'; free space is 'total-avail' and there is no 'avail'. The
    responses are kept verbatim in t/fixtures/powervault/ and the suite now
    reads them from disk, including the customer's measured 98.56% for the
    pool that previously read as 100% full.
  * POWERVAULT: a host belonging to no host group might still not have been
    found. The ME CLI prints ungrouped hosts in a separate block and which
    key that becomes in JSON is unknown - which for a single-node install is
    the default case, not an edge case. The list of keys is no longer what
    decides: every object in an ME answer names its own type in
    'object-name', and a row saying "object-name": "host" is collected
    whichever key it arrived under. The converse holds too - a row reached
    through a host key that names itself something else is that other thing,
    never a host.
  * docs/TESTING.md gained a section recording what that array established
    and what it did not.

